 have to respect each  other and even more than respecting each  other to echo aristotle we have to we  have to respect the truth that's a  crucial part i think of um humanistic  inquiry it's also a crucial part of  science for a very simple reason which  is that evolution filters out fact  recalcitrance over time if you don't  live in a way which respects the brute  facts and brute facts is perhaps the  right phrase here about how the world is  then you will die your species will die  out if you take disinfectant instead of  a vaccine for the virus you will die  it's as simple as that you'll get  selected out um it's not for nothing  that people talk of there being a darwin  award sometimes on the internet well the  darwin awards is an award for the it's  an award for getting yourself selected  out of the gene pool it's an award for  those who don't respect the facts  thanks very much and perfect timing  which is quite rare especially for a  philosopher i have to say so so good for  you um and andy what do you take of this  issue welcoming the challenge to facts  and reason and as sophie grace has  already pointed out  we have to say which challenges we we  welcome in which we don't it's not a  single question  yeah i think i agree i think there are  uh several different challenges uh to  facts and reason  um and i want to distinguish i want to  take a minute to distinguish two of them  that i think are particularly worth  mentioning one of them has been kind of  associated with left-wing progressives  typically in humanities departments and  it's a kind of relativist challenge the  other one is the thing that i think is  coming from the populists um and i'm  gonna say something about that uh next  so first the uh the challenge that comes  from the left from the progressives and  the academy is a kind of relativist  challenge that's grounded in  um the works of thomas kuhn for example  and the later wittgenstein  uh relativism about reason in this sense  is the idea that there are no standards  for reasoning that are really rational  that are really objectively the right  standards that you uh should adopt  across all contexts and all cultures uh  so there's no asymmetry uh really  between science and the norms of science  the norms that we adopt when we engage  in science as compared to astrology or  tea leaf reading  it's just that some norms are accepted  in some  cultures and some are different norms  are accepted in others  relativism about facts i think is this  kind of social constructivist idea that  facts are not chunks of reality that are  out there waiting to be discovered  rather the facts are something that we  project onto the world right um so  science again is not privileged as a  particularly effective way of coming to  know these independently existing facts  there's just different ways in which we  might construct them  so you might think that that kind of  challenge is to be welcomed right it was  you know when i was a grad student uh in  in the late 90s it was all the rage to  issue these kinds of challenges to fact  and reason and uh for many people this  came out of a kind of view that it was  aligned with progressive values in some  sense  so for example i think there were lots  of feminist critiques of science that  demonstrated the extent to which  sciences you know uh operates with  gender stereotypic uh biases  um and it seems like a natural extension  of this to say that it's not just the  you know the scientific research the  beliefs but the very logic and  scientific method are masculine  constructs  and this seems like some kind of radical  extension of the feminist project  second i think people thought that uh  relativism promotes tolerance in some  sense towards other ways of knowing  right because it allows us to validate  the belief systems of others we can say  for example that the azande belief in  witchcraft is you know true relative to  them and we can say that uh that you  know  belie